  "findings": [
    {
      "area": "Literal realOrderK power/log bound",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalKGlobalBudget.lean:44-215",
      "finding": "The existing Abel-normalized theorem is inverted at abs(alpha), including alpha=0. The q=1 moment mass is identified with the actual positive realOrderK value and paid by the same small-q estimate. Large-q exponential decay yields the global denominator. The final bound and its argument monotonicity have no supplied integral/asymptotic/spectral estimate."
    },
    {
      "area": "Actual transform and physical support",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalKPacketBudget.lean:43-159",
      "finding": "Uses the existing spectralTest and literal realOrderK/q on [Y/16,16Y]. Exact volume (255/16)*Y gives 255*kernelBudget(Y/16). The canonical G factor is 255*2*16=8160 with an additional 16^theta order debit. The actual mode integral factors with completedPacketSum still explicit."
    },
    {
      "area": "Physical 4*pi and squared A/J weight split",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalKPacketBudget.lean:163-237; Part3ActualExceptionalPhysicalNormalization.lean:37-66",
      "finding": "The squared split is derived using sqrt(X0)>=1; physicalScalePacket inserts 4*pi*sqrt(A*V*U)/(C*sqrt(Z)) and L=U/nu. The threshold ratio retains 16*pi^2. lambda>=975/4096 is explicit and only implies tau<=7/32 arithmetically; no actual spectral-gap instance is supplied."
    },
    {
      "area": "Opposite-sign factor and norm theorem boundary",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalPhysicalNormalization.lean:68-103",
      "finding": "The literal factor is (4/pi)*cos(pi*alpha/2), matching the exceptional specialization of Pascadi (3.18). Its norm bound is separate from the squared bare-K theorem; the normalized square has the extra (4/pi)^2. The fixed-level A range lemma is only scalar range inclusion. Positive-sign J-Bessel, actual A/J norm application and automorphic normalization remain open."
    },
    {
      "area": "Finite smoothing/Cauchy and coefficient energy",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalPhysicalNormalization.lean:107-167",
      "finding": "Triangle inequality and weighted Cauchy use c_k=norm(actualCoeff) and s_k=norm(completedPacketSum), with actualW(rho,sigma,0) supplied by the proved Fourier summability. E=sum c_k*s_k^2 remains explicit. No spectral/counting norm estimate is assumed or substituted. Arithmetic completed sum, signs, rows, carriers, common interval and physical phase remain unchanged; Fourier weights use the correctly inserted physical rho."
    },
    {
      "area": "Uniform smoothing constant",
      "source": "Part3ActualExceptionalPhysicalNormalization.lean:171-198",
      "finding": "B is chosen before every packet, A,C,D,x,eta,alpha and finite K. D>0 and V<=x^eta*C/D give rho<=x^eta. The consumed actual derivative/Fourier theorem pays actualW<=B*x^(12*eta) under x>=1, eta>=0 and abs(sigma)<=1. Explicit logarithmic Y and remaining coefficient energy costs are not silently absorbed."
    },
    {
      "area": "Original source and predecessor scope",
      "source": "Original manuscript116-255; medium completion299-405,454-484; H/I reports and registered production interfaces",
      "finding": "H supplies finite counted-polynomial algebra, I supplies the separate m=0 source, and existing Eq24-to-Eq25 code supplies real-expression algebra. These do not prove the missing nonzero spectral estimate. New code has no Eq24 or spectral-target premise and makes no KL/Friedrichs/finite-Hecke-to-automorphic identification."
    }
  ],
